# Copy to Makefile.local (git-ignored) to choose what `make build` signs with. Without a Makefile.local the build signs
# with the only "Developer ID Application" identity in the keychain, and ad hoc when there is none.
#
# A certificate's SHA-1 (`security find-identity -v -p codesigning`), or any part of its name; "-" signs ad hoc on purpose.
# A name that isn't in the keychain stops the build instead of signing ad hoc.
SIGN_IDENTITY := 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000

# What `make app` hands to `notarytool` to prove who is asking: one of the two forms below. `make app` refuses with neither,
# and each variable also works on the command line. Nothing here is a secret itself, but none of it goes in a tracked file.
#
# 1. A keychain profile, made once on the release Mac with `xcrun notarytool store-credentials <name>`. The profile holds the
#    credentials; only its name goes here. The profile cannot be read while the screen is locked.
NOTARY_PROFILE := example-notary-profile

# 2. An App Store Connect API key (a team key made in App Store Connect): the path to its .p8 file, the key's ID and the issuer's
#    UUID. This form works with the screen locked. All three go together, and when they are set they win over NOTARY_PROFILE.
#    Make does not expand ~, so spell the path out or build it from $(HOME).
# NOTARY_KEY := $(HOME)/keys/AuthKey_EXAMPLE.p8
# NOTARY_KEY_ID := EXAMPLEKEY1
# NOTARY_ISSUER := 00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000
